message:
I found something very interesting. Look at this code in `md_analyze_line`:

```c
#if defined __linux__ && !defined MD4C_USE_UTF16
    /* Recent glibc versions have superbly optimized strcspn(), even using
     * vectorization if available. */
    if(ctx->doc_ends_with_newline  &&  off < ctx->size) {
        while(TRUE) {
            off += (OFF) strcspn(STR(off), "\r\n");

            /* strcspn() can stop on zero terminator; but that can appear
             * anywhere in the Markfown input... */
            if(CH(off) == _T('\0'))
                off++;
            else
                break;
        }
    } else
#endif
```

This uses `strcspn` which stops at null bytes. The comment says "strcspn() can stop on zero terminator; but that can appear anywhere in the Markdown input..." and it handles this by checking `CH(off) == _T('\0')` and incrementing `off`. But there's a subtle issue: `strcspn` stops at the null terminator, but if the input has embedded null bytes, `strcspn` will stop at the first null byte. The code then increments `off` past the null byte and calls `strcspn` again. This should work correctly.

But wait - there's a potential issue. `strcspn` reads until it finds a character in the reject set OR a null terminator. If the input has embedded null bytes, `strcspn` stops at the first null byte. The code handles this by checking `CH(off) == _T('\0')` and incrementing `off`. But what if `off` reaches `ctx->size`? Then `CH(off)` reads `ctx->text[ctx->size]` which is out of bounds!

Actually, looking at the code more carefully:
```c
if(ctx->doc_ends_with_newline  &&  off < ctx->size) {
    while(TRUE) {
        off += (OFF) strcspn(STR(off), "\r\n");
        if(CH(off) == _T('\0'))
            off++;
        else
            break;
    }
}
```

If `off` reaches `ctx->size`, then `CH(off)` reads `ctx->text[ctx->size]` which is out of bounds. But `strcspn` would stop at the null terminator of the C string. If the input doesn't end with a null byte, `strcspn` would read past `ctx->size` looking for a null terminator or a newline character.

Wait, but the condition `ctx->doc_ends_with_newline` is checked. This is set to `(size > 0 && ISNEWLINE_(text[size-1]))`. So if the input ends with a newline, `strcspn` would find the newline within the input. But if the input has embedded null bytes, `strcspn` stops at the first null byte, and the code increments `off` past it. Then `strcspn` is called again from the new position.

The issue: if the input ends with a newline but has embedded null bytes, `strcspn` stops at the first null byte. The code increments `off` past it. Then `strcspn` is called again. This continues until `off` reaches `ctx->size`. At that point, `CH(off)` reads `ctx->text[ctx->size]` which is out of bounds.

But wait - the condition `off < ctx->size` is checked before the loop. Inside the loop, after `off += strcspn(...)`, `off` could be >= `ctx->size`. Then `CH(off)` reads out of bounds.

Actually, let me think about this more carefully. `strcspn(STR(off), "\r\n")` returns the number of characters until a `\r`, `\n`, or null terminator. If the input has embedded null bytes, `strcspn` stops at the first null byte. The code then checks `CH(off) == _T('\0')` and increments `off`. If `off` was at `ctx->size - 1` and `CH(off) == '\0'`, then `off` becomes `ctx->size`. Then the loop continues, and `strcspn(STR(ctx->size), "\r\n")` reads from `ctx->text[ctx->size]` which is out of bounds.

But actually, `strcspn` reads until it finds a null terminator. If `ctx->text[ctx->size]` is not null (which it isn't, since the input buffer is `size` bytes), `strcspn` would read past the buffer looking for a null terminator or newline. This is a buffer over-read.

